{
  "rationale": "Evidence. Iteration 5 changed only line_loss from last_direction to search_right, against iteration 4 (kd 0.5, kp 3.5, y = 0.07). Score moved from 64.17 to 64.01, which is within noise. Every track still had exactly 1 line-loss event per run, max_error stayed at 0.061 to 0.065 m (above the 0.06 m floor), and all 60 trials went off track. The recovery direction is therefore not the limiting factor. Across iterations 3 to 5, the forward sensor row (y 0.04 to 0.07) was the only change that moved the score materially (about +10 points). Lookahead is the lever that has paid off.\n\nDecision. Move the sensor row forward again, from y = 0.07 to y = 0.08 m, the top of the allowed range. Keep everything else as in iteration 5 (x = -0.04 to +0.04 at 0.02 spacing, sensor_size 0.012, weights -1, -0.5, 0, 0.5, 1, kp 3.5, ki 0, kd 0.5, base_speed 1.6, line_loss search_right). Sensor y does not change the signal scale, so the steering gain stays calibrated. Keeping search_right is neutral, since it matched last_direction within noise, and it avoids reverting a change without evidence.\n\nExpected result: max_error falls below about 0.06 m, line_loss_events drop toward 0, and progress rises above 0.2 on the s-bend and slalom. Hypotheses: (H1) a further 0.01 m of lookahead reduces the single line-loss event; (H2) if the gain is flat, the limit is not sensing lookahead and the next test is kd 0.8 or a lower base_speed (about 1.4) to cut entry speed into bends; (H3) if steering_oscillation rises above about 0.15, the stronger front signal is over-steering and kp should drop toward 2.5.\n\nMeasurements wanted: completion, progress, completion_time, max_error, rms_error, line_loss_events, steering_oscillation, control_effort, and controller_errors. Read line_loss_events and max_error first, then check whether any run gets past progress 0.5.",
